def bullet(text): doc.add_paragraph(text, style="List Bullet")

p=doc.add_paragraph(style="Title"); p.add_run("Distributed Object Storage Project")
p=doc.add_paragraph(); p.alignment=WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH.LEFT
r=p.add_run("Implementation and verification report"); r.bold=True; r.font.size=Pt(14); r.font.color.rgb=RGBColor(89,89,89)
doc.add_paragraph("Prepared for the course project review | 29 October 2026")
doc.add_heading("Executive summary", level=1)
doc.add_paragraph("This project builds an exam-scale distributed object-storage framework with content-addressed storage nodes, PostgreSQL object metadata, chunking and consistent-hash placement, replication and Reed-Solomon coding, an authenticated FastAPI gateway, a React console, job queues, monitoring, and deployment templates. The current repository contains the implementation, test and benchmark scripts, recorded reports, and task documentation.")
doc.add_paragraph("Verification evidence: the latest recorded automated suite passed 54 tests. Compose configuration validation passed. The most recent Docker image build did not complete because Docker returned an ‘exec format error’ during a base-image build step; therefore the latest deployment was not verified live. The cloud Kubernetes manifest is a template and has not been deployed to AWS, GCP, or Azure. Local metadata PostgreSQL remains a single instance.")

doc.add_heading("System architecture", level=1)
doc.add_paragraph("Clients use the React interface or API clients to reach the FastAPI gateway through Nginx. The gateway authenticates users with JWT, applies rate limits, and provides object, bucket, search, and cluster-status APIs. It delegates object storage and metadata operations to the metadata service. Metadata chunks content, selects storage nodes on a consistent-hash ring, records object manifests and placement state in PostgreSQL, and coordinates durability operations. Storage nodes persist hash-addressed chunks on disk and report heartbeats. RabbitMQ distributes repair, rebalance, and garbage-collection jobs to workers. Elasticsearch indexes searchable object metadata. Prometheus scrapes service metrics and Grafana presents the monitoring dashboard.")
for heading, description in [
    ("User interface and edge", "React and Vite provide the browser interface. Nginx accepts gateway traffic and proxies requests to the gateway replicas."),
    ("API", "The FastAPI gateway handles JWT authentication, rate limiting, streaming and range requests, search, and cluster status."),
    ("Metadata and durability", "The metadata API stores manifests, references, policies, placement, repair, and scrub state in PostgreSQL."),
    ("Data plane", "Storage node containers or Kubernetes StatefulSet pods store and verify content-addressed chunks on persistent disks."),
    ("Background services", "RabbitMQ queues maintenance jobs. Workers perform repair, rebalance, and garbage-collection actions. Elasticsearch indexes object metadata."),
    ("Observability", "Prometheus collects gateway, node, degraded-object, and repair metrics. Grafana displays them.")]:
    doc.add_heading(heading, level=2); doc.add_paragraph(description)

doc.add_heading("Work completed by task", level=1)
tasks=[
("Environment setup", "The development environment was organized around a Python virtual environment and a local Git repository. Runtime dependencies are pinned in requirement files so the API services and test tools can be recreated. Docker Compose defines the shared development infrastructure: Prometheus, Grafana, Kafka, and RabbitMQ, with persistent named volumes and local-only published ports. Grafana is provisioned with Prometheus as its data source. A `.env.example` documents configuration while the real `.env` remains private and is excluded from the submission package.", "The setup README records Python/Git/Docker/kubectl checks, virtual environment setup, Compose startup, health checks, and the command to attach a GitHub/GitLab remote. Docker Desktop Kubernetes was available locally in prior verification, but there is no configured cloud context.", "docs/environment/README.md; compose.yaml; requirements.txt; requirements.lock.txt; .env.example; .gitignore", "The setup report records successful dependency checks and healthy local services at the time of that run. No GitHub/GitLab remote was supplied, so the repository remains local. Cloud credentials were not configured."),
("Task 1 - Storage node", "Each storage node is a FastAPI service that accepts PUT, GET, HEAD, and DELETE operations for a chunk whose identifier is its lowercase SHA-256 digest. Uploads are streamed into a staging file, hashed while arriving, and only committed to the chunk directory if the digest matches. Reads verify a private disk snapshot before sending response bytes, preventing corrupted content from being returned as valid. Each node reports its ID, advertised address, free disk bytes, and chunk count on a short heartbeat interval. Compose gives nodes separate containers and persistent volumes.", "The service includes health checks, a chunk-size limit, atomic replacement on writes, integrity error handling, and per-digest request locks. Verification scripts exercise API operations, hash mismatch rejection, node separation, persistent data across restart, and deliberate on-disk tampering. The benchmark script creates synthetic many-small-file and few-large-file workloads, then checks stored/reassembled content.", "storage_node/app.py; Dockerfile; Dockerfile.offline; compose.storage.yaml; metadata_stub.py; scripts/verify_containers.py; scripts/benchmark_storage.py; reports/container-verification.json; reports/workloads.json", "Unit and container verification artifacts and generated workload results are present. The benchmark inputs are generated by the script rather than stored as bulk binary files. Task 1 covers node storage and client-side workload splitting; metadata manifests and durability policies were introduced in later tasks."),
("Task 2 - Chunking and placement", "The metadata service accepts object bytes and supports fixed-size chunking and content-defined chunking. Fixed chunks use the configured 4 MiB size. The rolling-hash chunker uses bounded chunk sizes to find content boundaries that remain more stable after insertions. PostgreSQL stores object bucket/key, size, version, chunking mode, ordered chunk manifest, tags, owner, and creation time. The chunk table tracks reference counts so duplicate content is stored once and physical data is removed when the final object reference is deleted. A consistent-hash ring with virtual nodes maps digests to live storage nodes.", "The placement-measure endpoint compares assignments before and after a node is added or removed and returns the number and percentage of hashes that move. The verification script covers object upload/download, manifests, deduplication and reference counting, content-defined chunking, and ring movement over a large generated hash set.", "metadata_service/chunking.py; metadata_service/ring.py; metadata_service/app.py; scripts/verify_metadata.py; reports/task-2-verification.json", "Algorithm tests and a live verification script are included. Placement measurement is a simulation endpoint; actual movement is performed later by the rebalance workflow. PostgreSQL is local and single-instance."),
("Task 3 - Replication and recovery", "Buckets can select a durability policy. Replication uses N copies with configurable write and read quorums, enforcing W + R > N. Erasure-coded buckets use Reed-Solomon data and parity fragments, including the example 4+2 layout, with per-fragment hashes and placement metadata. Metadata detects expired heartbeats, marks placements on failed nodes, and creates repair records. Repair reads healthy replicas or enough erasure fragments, reconstructs the logical chunk, regenerates the missing fragment, writes it to an eligible live node, and updates PostgreSQL. A periodic scrubber rereads stored fragments, detects invalid hashes, and schedules repairs. Reliability counters track storage and repair traffic.", "The verifier exercises replicated and erasure-coded storage, simulated failure, recovery, scrub detection, and repair. It also produces comparison data for physical storage overhead and repair traffic across workload types. Policy constraints require enough distinct live storage nodes before upload.", "metadata_service/reliability.py; metadata_service/app.py; scripts/verify_reliability.py; reports/task-3-verification.json", "Reliability algorithms and verification evidence are in the repository. Metadata still uses one PostgreSQL server; database-level high availability was not implemented. Repair also depends on enough healthy source copies/fragments remaining."),
("Task 4 - FastAPI gateway", "The gateway provides a user-facing object API and a basic service index. It issues and validates JWTs for the configured exam account and applies process-local rate limits. Protected routes support bucket creation/listing; streamed object upload; metadata lookup; search by prefix, size, date, and user tags; delete; full and single byte-range download; and cluster status including nodes and degraded objects. The gateway forwards object data to metadata without requiring clients to call internal storage nodes directly.", "The live gateway verification checks authorization failures and success, bucket/object lifecycle, streamed data, tags and search, range responses, cluster state, and deletion. A Postman collection mirrors the API flow and stores the returned access token for subsequent requests.", "gateway_service/app.py; scripts/verify_gateway.py; postman/task-4-gateway.postman_collection.json; reports/task-4-verification.json", "API tests, a live verifier, and Postman checks are supplied. Rate limits are held in each gateway process and are not shared between replicas. The exam login uses one configured account rather than a user identity system."),
("Task 5 - React interface and search", "The React/Vite application provides sign-in, bucket and object browsing, upload, download, delete, object search, and cluster visibility. It calls the authenticated gateway rather than accessing metadata or storage nodes directly. The gateway indexes object metadata in Elasticsearch, including searchable key/bucket/owner fields, size, dates, and user tags. Existing PostgreSQL metadata is backfilled when the gateway starts; successful writes update the index and deletes remove indexed documents.", "The frontend has standard lint/build commands and an interactive browser workflow. The gateway verifier checks search filters and object workflows at the API level; the browser workflow verifies the user interface against a running stack.", "frontend/src/; frontend/package.json; frontend/package-lock.json; frontend/Dockerfile; gateway_service/app.py; compose.storage.yaml; reports/task-5-verification.json", "The UI source, lockfile, API indexing, and instructions are present. Elasticsearch is a single-node development service. Browser checks and production UI build should be rerun when Docker builds successfully."),
("Task 6 - Load balancing and operations", "Nginx serves as the local entry point on port 8080 and proxies to two gateway replicas. RabbitMQ has separate durable repair, rebalancing, and garbage-collection queues. Metadata can publish jobs; workers consume them and call the matching metadata maintenance endpoint. A storage-node StatefulSet manifest defines four pods with per-pod PVCs plus liveness and readiness probes. Prometheus scrapes gateway throughput and latency, metadata degraded-object and repair counters, and per-node free space/chunk count. Grafana provisions an overview dashboard.", "Compose can scale gateways and workers. A test node profile is intended to add a storage node and cause the heartbeat path to queue rebalancing. Automated tests cover existing service logic and worker job routing; Compose syntax validation checks the service model.", "infra/nginx.conf; scripts/job_worker.py; compose.storage.yaml; deploy/kubernetes/storage-nodes.yaml; infra/prometheus.yml; infra/grafana/dashboards/; reports/", "The test suite passed 54 tests and Compose configuration passed validation. Live rollout was previously blocked by Docker engine/build failures. RabbitMQ management polling acknowledges on dequeue, so it does not have robust redelivery on worker crash. Local PostgreSQL remains a single point of failure."),
("Task 7", "The project materials reviewed contain no Task 7 specification, acceptance criteria, or task-specific README. No Task 7 implementation is claimed in this report.", "A task cannot be implemented or tested without acceptance criteria. This is recorded as a requirements gap rather than being inferred from adjacent tasks.", "No docs/task-7/README.md or Task 7-specific module was found in the repository listing.", "The professor's Task 7 requirements are needed to define and verify this task. Not verifiable from the current requirements."),
("Task 8 - Cloud deployment", "Dedicated Dockerfiles were added for storage, metadata, gateway, repair worker, and UI. Compose includes the UI service for local evaluation. A cloud-neutral Kubernetes manifest describes the storage StatefulSet and persistent volumes, a gateway Deployment, a cloud LoadBalancer Service, and a CPU-based HorizontalPodAutoscaler with 2–10 gateway replicas. Metadata heartbeat handling was updated to queue a rebalance job when a storage node first registers or returns from failure.", "The README documents image build/push commands, registry placeholders, Kubernetes secrets, deployment commands, and the intended check for storage-node addition and rebalance. Cloud-specific provisioning is left to the selected provider account and Kubernetes context.", "storage_node/Dockerfile; metadata_service/Dockerfile; gateway_service/Dockerfile; scripts/Dockerfile.worker; frontend/Dockerfile; compose.storage.yaml; deploy/kubernetes/task8-cloud.yaml; docs/task-8/README.md", "Tests and Compose config validation pass. Docker build failed locally at a base image RUN step with ‘exec format error’; no service images were successfully verified or pushed. No AWS/GCP/Azure cluster was provisioned. The Kubernetes template still requires registry names, secrets, managed PostgreSQL, and in-cluster metadata/RabbitMQ endpoints.")]
for title, implementation, detail, files, status in tasks:
    doc.add_heading(title, level=2)
    doc.add_paragraph(implementation)
    doc.add_paragraph(detail)
    p=doc.add_paragraph(); p.add_run("Key files. ").bold=True; p.add_run(files)
    p=doc.add_paragraph(); p.add_run("Verification and status. ").bold=True; p.add_run(status)

doc.add_heading("Problems encountered by task", level=1)
doc.add_paragraph("This section records environment and implementation difficulties documented during the work. A design limitation is identified as such; it is not presented as a defect that was fixed.")
problems = [
    ("Environment setup", "Docker Desktop’s Linux engine at one point returned HTTP 500 on the Docker API ping, preventing Compose from reaching the server. Switching to the `desktop-linux` context confirmed the selected endpoint but did not itself repair the engine. The engine later recovered. The repository also has no GitHub/GitLab remote because no destination repository was provided."),
    ("Task 1 - Storage node", "The local BuildKit cache produced `exec format error` and missing-snapshot errors during image builds. The project documentation records an offline Dockerfile fallback that reuses downloaded dependencies. This is a local build-environment issue; it does not change the storage API design. Large benchmark runs can take longer than unit checks, so the benchmark is a separate command."),
    ("Task 2 - Chunking and placement", "Python package downloads and Windows/Linux wheel compatibility made image preparation less reliable on the local machine. The documented workaround downloads the psycopg binary wheel for the Linux container and builds with the offline Dockerfile. Placement-measurement results are simulations over generated hashes; Task 2 itself does not move already-stored chunks."),
    ("Task 3 - Reliability", "Full-size erasure-coding comparisons are computationally expensive for the pure-Python Reed-Solomon implementation. The recorded quick verifier therefore uses bounded sample workloads, and its estimated repair traffic should not be interpreted as a full-scale throughput benchmark. Repairs also require enough surviving replicas or independent fragments to reconstruct the source."),
    ("Task 4 - Gateway", "The gateway uses one configured exam identity and keeps rate-limit counters in process memory. With multiple replicas, limits are not shared. Elasticsearch indexing occurs after database/object writes; an indexing failure can return an API error after the object has already been committed. These are known design limits rather than resolved deployment faults."),
    ("Task 5 - React console", "The browser UI depends on the gateway being reachable at the configured local API URL and on the gateway CORS allow-list matching the local frontend origin. Therefore the API verifier alone does not prove the browser flow; sign-in, upload, search, download, and delete still need an interactive check after the full stack starts."),
    ("Task 6 - Operations and monitoring", "The first rollout could not be verified while Docker Desktop returned HTTP 500; later a build attempt failed with `exec format error`. Kubernetes dry-run also encountered a TLS handshake timeout to the local cluster API. The local metadata database remains a single PostgreSQL instance, and worker polling acknowledges a RabbitMQ message when dequeued, so a worker crash can lose that job. These gaps should be resolved before claiming production-level availability."),
    ("Task 7", "No Task 7 requirements were supplied in the project materials reviewed, so no implementation or verification could be planned without inventing scope. The missing specification is the blocker."),
    ("Task 8 - Cloud deployment", "The per-service and Kubernetes configurations are templates, but local Docker image builds have not completed reliably, cloud CLIs/accounts were not configured, and no provider cluster was supplied. Consequently, images were not pushed, a cloud cluster was not created, and HPA or cloud load-balancer behavior was not demonstrated. The manifest also expects managed PostgreSQL and metadata/RabbitMQ services to be wired into the Kubernetes network.")]
for title, problem in problems:
    doc.add_heading(title, level=2)
    doc.add_paragraph(problem)

